package Lessons

use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

class SceneTerrain2 is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("experiment/Scenes/terrain.html")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews. An experiment, so it stays
        // out of search: the real lesson is the one to find.
        SetDescription("Learn to add a terrain to a 3D scene in Quorum Studio, select it, read its properties, and find the files it is saved in.")
        SetSearchable(false)

        Title("Terrain in a Scene")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("Most 3D games and simulations happen on the ground: hills to climb, valleys
to drive through, and flat land to build on. In 3D graphics this ground is
called "):Term("terrain"):Text(". In this tutorial, you will add a terrain to a 3D scene in
Quorum Studio, select it, read its properties, and look at the files Quorum Studio
saves for it. Along the way you will see that a terrain is really a large
set of numbers, one height for each point on the ground, split into square
pieces called regions so that big worlds stay manageable.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Add a terrain to a 3D scene, and select and delete it")
        ListItem("Understand that a terrain is height data split into regions")
        ListItem("Find a terrain's files in a project, and describe what each one holds")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Think of an outdoor place you know well, such as a park, a school field or
a hill near your home. How would you describe its shape to someone who has
never seen it, using only numbers? What would you measure, and how far
apart would your measurements need to be?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/scenes/Terrain/InAScene/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_DATA_COLLECTION_AND_PREPARATION_22)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_4)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        Paragraph("A terrain is the ground under a 3D scene. Instead of being one big model
made by hand, it is stored as "):Strong("data"):Text(": a grid of heights. Quorum measures
the height of the ground at evenly spaced points, called "):Term("samples"):Text(", and
draws a smooth surface through them. The closer together the samples are,
the more detail the ground can have, and the more numbers there are to
store.")
        BeforeYouStart()
        AddingTerrain()
        TerrainStaysAtOrigin()
        MovingAroundTerrainKeyboard()
        MovingCameraKeyboard()
        SelectingTerrain()
        TerrainSectionPropertiesPanel()
        Regions()
        FilesOnDisk()
        DeletingUndoingRedoing()
    end

    private action BeforeYouStart
        Subsection("Before you start")
        Paragraph("A terrain needs three things. If one is missing, Quorum Studio says why
instead of adding it:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Vulkan"):Text(", the graphics system terrains are drawn with. Quorum Studio
must be running with Vulkan. Otherwise you hear "):Quoted("A terrain needs Vulkan, so it can't be
added here.")
        ListItem():Strong("A project."):Text(" A terrain's files are saved in the scene's project. A scene
opened on its own gives "):Quoted("A terrain is saved in the scene's project, but
this scene isn't in a project.")
        ListItem():Strong("A 3D layer"):Text(" in the scene. Otherwise you hear "):Quoted("A terrain needs a 3D
layer, and this scene has none.")
        Paragraph("If you have not made a scene yet, see
"):Link("Creating a Scene", "/experiment/Scenes/create.html"):Text(".")
    end

    private action AddingTerrain
        Subsection("Adding a terrain")
        Paragraph("Terrain is in the palette, in the "):Strong("Basic 3D"):Text(" group, next to the cube,
sphere and other shapes. Its description, read by a screen reader, is
"):Quoted("Ground for the scene, made of heightmap regions. It starts flat, 128 by
128 units.")
        Paragraph("There are two ways to add it:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("With the keyboard:"):Text(" move to Terrain in the palette and press
"):Key("Enter"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("With the mouse:"):Text(" drag Terrain into the scene and let go. The terrain
is added only when you drop it; dragging over the scene and back off adds
nothing.")
        Paragraph("Unlike other items, a terrain does not show a preview to place first. It is
added at once, flat, at the origin. Quorum Studio saves its files straight away,
selects it and says, for example:")
        Paragraph():Quoted("Placed Terrain, flat, 128.0 by 128.0 units, saved in
Scenes/Terrains/Terrain1.")
        Paragraph("A new terrain is flat and plain. Later tutorials shape it, generate hills
and mountains on it, and paint it with materials such as grass and rock.")
    end

    private action TerrainStaysAtOrigin
        Subsection("A terrain stays at the origin")
        Paragraph("Other items can be moved, turned and scaled. A terrain can't. Its heights
are positions in the world, and games use them to work out where the ground
is, so it always stays at the origin. If you press a move, turn or scale
key with a terrain selected, it stays where it is, and the first time you
hear:")
        Paragraph():DoubleQuote():Output("A terrain stays at the origin. It can't be moved, turned or scaled."):DoubleQuote()
        Paragraph("Instead of moving a terrain, you move the other items onto it. A later
tutorial, "):Link("Shaping Terrain", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainShape.html"):Text(", shows
how the move keys take on a new job once you choose a terrain tool.")
    end

    private action MovingAroundTerrainKeyboard
        Subsection("Moving around a terrain with the keyboard")
        Paragraph("Everything you can do on a terrain with the mouse, you can also do with the
keyboard. With nothing selected, the arrow keys move the scene editor's
"):Term("cursor"):Text(", a small marker, around the 3D grid:")
        Table("Keys for moving around the scene", "media/scenes/Terrain/InAScene/MoveKeys.csv")
        Paragraph("When you let go of an arrow key, a screen reader says what is at the
cursor's spot and where it is. To select the terrain, move the cursor down
to ground level and press "):Key("Enter"):Text(". If something stands on the ground there,
"):Key("Tab"):Text(" moves between the items in that spot. With a terrain selected, the
arrow keys don't move it, because a terrain stays at the origin. Once you
choose a terrain tool, they move the tool's brush over the ground instead,
as "):Link("Shaping Terrain", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainShape.html"):Text(" shows.")
    end

    private action MovingCameraKeyboard
        Subsection("Moving the camera with the keyboard")
        Paragraph("The view of the scene comes from the scene editor's "):Strong("camera"):Text(". With the
mouse, hold the right button and drag to look around, hold the middle
button and drag to orbit around a point, hold "):Key("Shift"):Text(" and the middle button
to pan, and use the scroll wheel to zoom.")
        Paragraph("With the keyboard, you move the camera by changing its properties:")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("Press "):Keys("Alt", "F"):Text(" ("):Keys("Option", "F"):Text(" on Mac) to select the scene editor's camera.")
        ListItem("Press "):Keys("Ctrl", "P"):Text(" ("):Keys("Cmd", "P"):Text(" on Mac) to move into the Properties panel.")
        ListItem("The camera's section has its "):Strong("distance"):Text(" from the point it looks at,
its "):Strong("direction"):Text(" as a yaw (turning left and right) and a pitch (looking
up and down), its "):Strong("zoom"):Text(", and its "):Strong("field of view"):Text(". "):Key("Tab"):Text(" to a field,
type a new value, and press "):Key("Enter"):Text(".")
        ListItem("Press "):Key("Escape"):Text(" to go back to the scene.")
        Paragraph("For example, changing the pitch tilts the camera to look further down at
the terrain or further across it, and a larger distance shows more of it at
once. With nothing selected, "):Keys("Ctrl", "Shift", "2"):Text(" says which way the camera
faces, so you can check it after each change. These keys work the
same in every scene, terrain or not; the full list is on
"):Link("Navigation and Keys", "/experiment/Scenes/navigate.html"):Text(".")
    end

    private action SelectingTerrain
        Subsection("Selecting a terrain")
        Paragraph("You can select a terrain in three ways:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("from the "):Strong("scene tree"):Text(", where it is listed once, by its name;")
        ListItem("by "):Strong("clicking the ground"):Text(" in the 3D view. If an item stands on the
ground, such as a cube, clicking the item selects the item, not the
ground;")
        ListItem("with the "):Strong("scene cursor:"):Text(" move the cursor to ground level and press
"):Key("Enter"):Text(".")
        Paragraph("A selected terrain gets a thin border around its edges.")
    end

    private action TerrainSectionPropertiesPanel
        Subsection("The Terrain section of the Properties panel")
        Paragraph("When a terrain is selected, the Properties panel on the right of the scene
tab shows its sections. Press "):Keys("Cmd", "P"):Text(" on Mac or "):Keys("Ctrl", "P"):Text(" on Windows to
move into the panel, "):Key("Tab"):Text(" to move through it, and "):Key("Escape"):Text(" to go
back to the scene. (The panel is introduced in
"):Link("Properties", "/experiment/Scenes/properties.html"):Text(".)")
        Paragraph("The first section is "):Strong("Terrain"):Text(". It starts with the terrain's "):Strong("Name"):Text(",
which you can change. Below it is a line for each fact about the terrain.
"):Key("Tab"):Text(" stops on each line, so a screen reader reads it. For a new terrain
they read:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():DoubleQuote():Output("Size: 128.0 by 128.0 units"):DoubleQuote()
        ListItem():DoubleQuote():Output("Regions: 4, each 64 by 64 samples"):DoubleQuote()
        ListItem():DoubleQuote():Output("Sample spacing: 1.0 units"):DoubleQuote()
        ListItem():DoubleQuote():Output("Ground height: 0.0 to 0.0"):DoubleQuote()
        ListItem():DoubleQuote():Output("Folder: Scenes/Terrains/Terrain1"):DoubleQuote()
        Paragraph("The ground height is the lowest and highest point on the terrain. It is 0
to 0 now because the terrain is flat. Watch it change when you shape the
ground in "):Link("Shaping Terrain", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainShape.html"):Text(".")
        Paragraph("The section ends with a button, "):Strong("Grow by one region on every side"):Text(", which
adds a ring of flat regions around the terrain. Regions are explained
below.")
        Paragraph("After the Terrain section come four more:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Materials"):Text(", for the ground's look
("):Link("Terrain Materials and Painting", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainMaterials.html"):Text(");")
        ListItem():Strong("Brush"):Text(", for shaping it
("):Link("Shaping Terrain", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainShape.html"):Text(");")
        ListItem():Strong("Generate"):Text(", for making ground from rules
("):Link("Generating Terrain", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainGenerate.html"):Text(");")
        ListItem():Strong("Physics"):Text(", for how the terrain acts in a running game, such as whether
things collide with it and how slippery it is
("):Link("Terrain in a Game", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainGames.html"):Text(").")
    end

    private action Regions
        Subsection("Regions")
        Paragraph("A large world could have millions of samples, so a terrain is split into
square "):Term("regions"):Text(". Each region is a small grid of samples with its own
heightmap file. A game can then load only the regions near the player, which
keeps very big worlds manageable. A new terrain in Quorum Studio has four
regions, two by two, each 64 by 64 samples, with the samples 1 unit apart.
That makes it 128 by 128 units, with the origin in the middle.")
        Paragraph("Regions are numbered by where they sit. Region 0, 0 starts at the origin
and covers the next 64 units along x and z; region -1, 0 is the one just
before it along x, and so on. You can add regions to make the world bigger,
or remove ones you don't need, with tools covered in
"):Link("Generating Terrain", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainGenerate.html"):Text(".")
    end

    private action FilesOnDisk
        Subsection("The files on disk")
        Paragraph("Quorum Studio keeps a scene's own files in the project's "):Strong("Scenes"):Text(" folder. Each
terrain gets a folder under "):Code("Scenes/Terrains"):Text(": the first is "):Code("Terrain1"):Text(", the
next "):Code("Terrain2"):Text(", and so on. A new terrain's folder holds:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Code("terrain.json"):Text(", a small text file describing the terrain;")
        ListItem("one image for each region, named for the region's place in the grid of
regions: "):Code("region_-1_-1.png"):Text(", "):Code("region_-1_0.png"):Text(", "):Code("region_0_-1.png"):Text(" and
"):Code("region_0_0.png"):Text(".")
        Paragraph("Open "):Code("terrain.json"):Text(" in Quorum Studio. It is plain data, much like this:")
        JsonExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/SceneTerrain/terrain.json")
        Paragraph("Each region's image holds its heights. Every pixel is one sample. Its color
is not a picture of the ground: the red, green and blue values together
store a number that says how far the sample's height is between the
region's lowest and highest heights, which "):Code("terrain.json"):Text(" lists. That is
why an image of hilly ground can look like odd stripes of color.")
        Paragraph("The scene file itself, the "):Code(".qs"):Text(" file, only refers to the terrain's
"):Code("terrain.json"):Text(", along with its name and its physics settings. Saving the
scene saves the terrain's files too.")
    end

    private action DeletingUndoingRedoing
        Subsection("Deleting, undoing and redoing")
        Paragraph("A terrain is deleted like any item: select it and press "):Key("Delete"):Text(" or
"):Key("Backspace"):Text(". Quorum Studio's Undo and Redo bring it back or remove it again.
Deleting a terrain doesn't delete its folder. Quorum Studio never deletes files
from a project when it saves, so the folder stays until you remove it.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you and a partner make a terrain out of numbers and read
each other's worlds from the numbers alone. The goal is to understand that a
terrain is a grid of heights, which is exactly what Quorum stores. Each of
you takes a piece of squared paper and marks out an area about 6 by 6
squares. In each square, write a height from 0 (a lake) to 9 (a mountain
peak). Don't draw anything else: just numbers. Then swap papers. Using only
the numbers, describe your partner's world out loud: where are the hills,
the valleys and the flat places? Your partner then says what they imagined,
and you compare.")
        Paragraph("Your paper is a heightmap, and each square is one sample, just like one
point in a Quorum terrain. Together, discuss:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("What would change if the squares were twice as far apart? Half as far?")
        ListItem("If your world were 1,000 squares wide, how could you split it into pieces
so a game only needs part of it at once?")
        ListItem("Write a short data dictionary for "):Code("terrain.json"):Text(": for each field, its
name, its type, and what values make sense.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("A terrain is a set of heights, measured at evenly spaced samples and split
into square regions, each saved as its own image, with "):Code("terrain.json"):Text(" to
describe them. What did you notice about the trade-off between detail and
the amount of data to store? In the next tutorial,
"):Link("Terrain Materials and Painting", "/experiment/Scenes/terrainMaterials.html"):Text(",
you will give the ground its look: grass, rock, dirt and sand.")
    end
end
